## THIS IS AUDIT REVISION $CF_AUDIT_ROUND — an auditor and an independent skeptic judged your document

Your document `$CF_AUDIT_DOCUMENT` is committed and passed the gate. Round $CF_AUDIT_ROUND's review judged its cut against the rules above and found what is listed in `.tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.verdicts.json`: `verdicts`, each with an `id` (`r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND-v001`, …), the `item` it names in the snapshot the review read (`.tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.document.json`), the `verdict`, the `rule` (the cut rules above, numbered 1 to 10 as they come: the test, units, displays, inside a sentence, lists, proofs, context, conjuncts, the subject, PD9) and an `action`. "This session" below names the verdicts this session applies, at most {{verdicts_per_session}}{{#if audit_skips}} by weight (a `{{skip_verdict}}` verdict weighs the more of its statements and the math paragraphs under its skip, any other verdict 1){{/if}}{{#if audit_gaps}}, a `{{gap_verdict}}` verdict the more of 1 and its statements{{/if}}; Read the file in parts when it is long. Apply them; do not re-cut the document. You do not rewrite the document by hand either: every change is an operation that cites the verdict it applies, and a change no operation produces is found and judged against the revision.

1. **Write the operations.** Work through your verdicts in document order. For each one you apply, add its operations to `.tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.ops.json`, each citing `"verdict": "<its id>"`; a neighbour's end or start that a verdict forces cites `"neighbour_of": "<its id>"`. Change nothing else: a cut no verdict names is the reviewed cut, so leave it. Items name the passages of the snapshot, as the verdicts do.

```json
{"schema": "{{audit_schema}}", "kind": "ops", "round": $CF_AUDIT_ROUND, "source_id": "$CF_AUDIT_SOURCE",
 "ops": [
   {"op": "merge", "keep": "In-text #3fa91c07", "drop": "In-text #0000c0de", "verdict": "r1-v001"},
   {"op": "split", "item": "In-text #00c0ffee", "at": {"exact": "Moreover"}, "verdict": "r1-v002",
    "new": {"statement": "…", "passage_kind": "theorem", "scope": "formal_math", "prominence": "supporting"}},
   {"op": "set", "item": "In-text #0badf00d", "fields": {"passage_kind": "theorem"}, "verdict": "r1-v003"}]}
```

   The operations are {{edit_ops}}, and the fields they set {{edit_fields}}:
   - `merge {keep, drop, fields?}`: `drop` (an item or a list) joins `keep`, which starts at the earlier start (with that passage's quote and finder), ends where the one ending later ended, and keeps its own other fields, then `fields`;
   - `split {item, at, new, earlier?}`: a new passage starts at the quote `at` inside `item` and ends where `item` ended; `new` gives its `statement`, `passage_kind`, `scope` and `prominence` (and may give `section` and `fragment`); `item` loses its `until` unless `earlier` gives one;
   - `set {item, fields}`: `null` removes `until`, `shape` and `conjuncts` and empties `concept`; `quote` moves the start; `quote_end` cuts the quote's `exact` after its first occurrence of those words;
   - `add {after, quote, fields}`: a passage from text no passage holds, after `after` (null: first), `fields` as a split's `new`;
   - `drop {item}`.
{{#if audit_skips}}
   - `unskip {skip, keep?}`: the skip `skip` (its id in the snapshot's numbering, `r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND-s<nnn>`) gives up its text but the pieces `keep` names, `[{"span": [a, b], "reason"?}]`, each inside it, disjoint and in order (a piece without a reason keeps the skip's): no `keep` drops it, one piece shrinks it, two split it. No operation adds or widens a skip.
{{/if}}

   A passage you scope out of `formal_math` becomes `supporting` and loses `shape` and `conjuncts`. A quote follows the document's rule: verbatim from the layer, starting at a word.

   **A `revert` verdict** (rule 0, origin `collateral`) undoes a change no operation of the last revision produced, which the check judged harmful. Its `action` names the entry (`revert collateral r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND-x001 (…)`) in `.tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.collateral.json`, whose `before` is the passage as it was and `after` the passage as it is. Put `before` back with operations that cite the `revert` verdict: a `hand_edit` with both is a `set` on `after`'s item of its `fields`, to their `before` values; one with only `before` (a passage lost) is an `add` with `before`'s quote and fields; one with only `after` (a passage added) is a `drop` of `after`'s item; a `moved` passage is a `set` on `after`'s item of the `until` (its last words) that ends it where `before`'s span ended.
{{#if audit_skips}}

   **A `{{skip_verdict}}` verdict** names a skip of the snapshot (`skip`, and `skips` when a carried verdict's `span` now holds several or none) and the statements the review found under it (`statements`, a proposal: cut by the rules above where they differ), or none when the skip rule raised it. Read the skip whole first (the command in step 2). Then write: an `unskip` of each skip it names (once: a skip two verdicts name is one `unskip`, citing either), with `keep` only for pieces of furniture, their spans copied from the rendering's `¶` lines; one `add` per statement, `scope: formal_math`, the kind the verdict proposes (a step that asserts a fact or an equation is `theorem`, one that defines `definition`), `prominence: supporting`, and a `statement` that reads on its own (a context sentence opens the statement that uses it); one `add` with `scope: expository` for each math paragraph of the freed text that no statement holds, since narration inside a proof or a worked example is an expository passage, never a skip (the coverage floor needs it covered, and C6 forbids skipping it); and, when it names a `merge_with`, the `set` of `until` on that passage that takes in the skipped text. Every operation cites the verdict. A `skip_edit` collateral entry is reverted with an `unskip` when a skip was added or widened by hand: the `revert` verdict's `action` names the skip as the snapshot numbers it (`r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND-s<nnn>`, the id the `unskip` takes; the entry's own `skip` is the round before's) and the `keep` that shrinks it back; no operation puts back a skip removed or narrowed by hand, so report that `revert` verdict `not_applied`, why: no operation restores a skip.
{{/if}}{{#if audit_gaps}}

   **A `{{gap_verdict}}` verdict** names a gap of the snapshot (`gap`, and `gaps` when a carried verdict's `span` now holds several or none), the statements the review found in it (`statements`, a proposal: cut by the rules above where they differ) and `after`, the passage before it. Write one `add` per statement, `after` that passage, `scope: formal_math`, the kind the verdict proposes, `prominence: supporting`, and a `statement` that reads on its own (a notation passage says what its symbol names); and, when it names a `merge_with`, the `set` of `until` on that passage that takes in the gap's text. Text of the gap that states nothing stays as it is. Every operation cites the verdict. To see the gap: `{{passage_command}} '<gap id>' --rule {{audit_rule}} --document .tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.document.json`.
{{/if}}
2. **Apply, re-key, gate.** Run the edit, the id tool and the gate's check:

```bash
{{edit_command}}{{#if audit_skips}} --rule {{audit_rule}}{{/if}} --document $CF_AUDIT_DOCUMENT --ops .tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.ops.json --base .tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.document.json --verdicts .tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.verdicts.json --write
{{ids_command}} --source-dir {{sources_dir}}/$CF_AUDIT_SOURCE --document $CF_AUDIT_DOCUMENT --write
{{gate_command}} --findings $CF_AUDIT_DOCUMENT
```

   The edit applies the whole ops file to the snapshot every time, so after a refusal fix the operation and run all three again. Without `--write` the edit is a dry run that prints what it would do. To see a passage of the document with the layer around it: `{{passage_command}} '<item>' --document $CF_AUDIT_DOCUMENT`{{#if audit_skips}}; to see a skip of the snapshot whole, `{{passage_command}} '<skip id>' --rule {{audit_rule}} --document .tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.document.json`{{/if}}.
3. **Gate refusals.** A verdict the gate refuses after a correct application: take its operations out, and record it as `gate_refused` with the gate's refusal line, verbatim, as `gate_line`.
4. **Disputes.** A verdict the cut rules as written do not support: do not apply it, and record it as `disputed`, with the rule and at most {{action_words}} words in `why`. A fresh skeptic rules on it. Do not dispute a verdict because applying it is work, and do not argue any verdict you applied.
5. **Report.** `.tend/audit/r$CF_AUDIT_ROUND.repair.json`, one entry per verdict of this session beside the earlier sessions' entries, so that after the last session it holds one for every verdict of the round:

```json
{"schema": "{{audit_schema}}", "kind": "repair", "round": $CF_AUDIT_ROUND, "source_id": "$CF_AUDIT_SOURCE",
 "entries": [
   {"verdict_id": "r1-v001", "status": "applied", "why": ""},
   {"verdict_id": "r1-v004", "status": "gate_refused", "why": "the merge crosses a list item",
    "gate_line": "<the gate's refusal line, verbatim>"}]}
```

   `status` is one of {{repair_statuses}}; any but `applied` gives its `why`.
6. **Commit and finish.** Run the validator lines "This session" gives. Commit only your document and the task file, never anything under `.tend/`, then finish as the task prompt above says.

The ops and repair files may already hold entries: an earlier session's, or your own before the gate refused a commit. Keep them, and add yours.
